1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,700
Meet Voice Pilot.

2
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:07,830
It lets you inspect a unit by talking, so your hands stay free for the flashlight and the outlet tester.

3
00:00:09,029 --> 00:00:13,949
You talk through your earbuds. The app turns your words into an NSPIRE finding,

4
00:00:13,750 --> 00:00:17,899
presses the same button you would, and tells you what it recorded.

5
00:00:19,100 --> 00:00:24,429
Here's a real example. In the kitchen, say: outlet dead, and GFCI won't trip,

6
00:00:24,230 --> 00:00:27,489
and cabinet door broken. It records all three,

7
00:00:27,289 --> 00:00:32,490
picks the Unit area from the room, and reads them back with their severity.

8
00:00:33,689 --> 00:00:38,620
Life-threatening findings are called out with their twenty-four hour deadline.

9
00:00:38,420 --> 00:00:40,810
If anything is wrong, just say undo.

10
00:00:42,009 --> 00:00:48,150
Still talking HQS? That's fine. It understands HQS wording and item numbers,

11
00:00:47,950 --> 00:00:53,160
and it explains when an old HQS fail isn't an NSPIRE deficiency.

12
00:00:54,359 --> 00:00:58,460
When a phrase is unusual, it asks Jev, a fast classifier.

13
00:00:58,259 --> 00:01:02,080
If neither is sure, it asks you, instead of guessing.

14
00:01:03,280 --> 00:01:06,880
Phrases it misses are saved without personal details,

15
00:01:06,680 --> 00:01:11,570
labeled overnight by an AI on your own server, and once you approve them,

16
00:01:11,370 --> 00:01:13,750
the app recognizes them next time.

17
00:01:14,950 --> 00:01:19,150
You confirm every finding. Voice Pilot just saves you the typing.
